WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.600
A frozen world model assumes every command is executed as sent.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.700
But the actuator may be off in a way we don't know.

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.200
ACTUS estimates the shift with the same frozen model and replans, without retraining.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.900
Visual planning uses a frozen latent world model and sampling-based MPC.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.400
A miscalibrated actuator transforms every command. We call this an actuation shift.

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.300
The frozen model cannot tell that anything is wrong.

7
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.200
The gap between prediction and observation is the residual.

8
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.200
We ask the model what would happen if the command had passed through T(θ).

9
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.700
The hypothesis that fits best tells us how the actuator is off.

10
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.700
A shift moves the prediction along only K directions, one JVP in each direction.

11
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.500
The residual contains both the shift and the model's own error.

12
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.900
Projecting the residual onto these directions gives the shift score:
a noisy measurement of the shift.

13
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.300
Why don't we use the prediction error? It sees a shift only at second order.

14
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.800
On 124 PushT shifts, the score detects what energy misses, as our theory predicted.

15
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.900
Pooling the score over a window gives a Rao test.

16
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.700
Its power depends on one number, λ:
the information collected about the shift.

17
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:21.600
254 shifts on three benchmarks fall on one predicted curve, regardless of generator,
size or task, so detection is known before any shift is seen.

18
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.800
Detect. The pooled score crosses the threshold two steps after the shift.

19
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.100
Estimate. Gauss–Newton runs on the same score and gives Cramér–Rao error bars.

20
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.900
Attribute. The largest coordinate is rotation.

21
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.600
Compensate. Each plan passes through T(δ̂)⁻¹ before it is executed.

22
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:44.900
All methods start from the same weights. ACTUS raises success
from 0.650 to 0.862 on PushT and from 0.378 to 0.432 on Cube.

23
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:51.800
This matches an oracle given the true shift,
while five test-time adaptation methods gain at most 2.3 points.

24
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.100
The remaining Cube gap comes mostly from the command box
and the blocks before the alarm.

25
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.700
The same procedure works on DINO-WM without retuning.

26
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:07.800
A real SO-101 arm with two cameras.
Each camera has its own world model, trained on nominal data and then frozen.

27
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.400
Here the base joint is off by 10°, but the encoders still read 0°,
so the arm's own sensors miss it.

28
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.200
From the wrist camera, ACTUS estimates 10.1° and turns the joint back.

29
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.000
Reach error drops from 36–38 mm to 3.0–5.5 mm, about the arm's repeatability.

30
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.300
We sealed three random shifts before the session. Here is one reach.

31
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.700
ACTUS estimated the shift from 50 exploration episodes.

32
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.300
Final frames of two reaches, alternating.
Without compensation the wrist view jumps, and with ACTUS it barely moves.

33
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.200
Distance from the nominal end point, over all 64 seed pairs per shift.

34
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.900
Error falls from 9.8–12.5 mm to 1.9–2.5 mm,
92–94% of the reduction achieved with the true shift.

